[THOUGHT] The patient is a 55-year-old male with acute severe abdominal pain, borderline hypotension (100/68 mmHg), tachycardia (97 bpm), and inability to localize pain or answer functional questions due to distress. This clinical picture strongly suggests an intra-abdominal surgical emergency. In men this age, key considerations include:  
- Abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) rupture — classically presents with sudden severe pain (often back/flank or diffuse), hypotension, and tachycardia; may lack clear localization early;  
- Mesenteric ischemia — especially with atrial fibrillation or hypercoagulable state;  
- Perforated peptic ulcer or diverticulitis;  
- Bowel obstruction or volvulus.  

Given the hemodynamic instability and acuity, imaging is urgently needed. Ultrasound is rapid, non-invasive, and highly sensitive for AAA — and is widely available in most acute settings. It can also assess for free fluid (suggesting hemorrhage or perforation). I will request abdominal ultrasound as the next critical test.

[TEST] Abdominal ultrasound (focused on aorta, bowel, gallbladder, and presence of free fluid)

